// The other half: more arguments than there is anywhere to put.
//
// The standard library's own signatures make this work on methods too, which is
// where it matters most — `upper` takes nothing beyond the string it is called
// on, and nothing could know that until the library said so.
fn one(a) { a }

one(1, 2)
"abc".upper(1)
[1, 2].len(0)
